<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagasae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pagasae was an ancient port town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Gulf of Pagasae</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volos">Volos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It served as the harbour of Pherae, whose tyrants drew strength from its trade and shipping, and myth made it the place where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a> was built. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> took it in 352 BCE, and it later lost importance to the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>, founded in 294 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagasae.jpg" alt="Pagasae" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thessaly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
